  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a MileIQ Alternative",
    "body": "Start with the number of business drives in a typical month. MileIQ Free covers 40 drives a month, Everlance Basic covers 30 automatically detected trips, and Driversnote Free reports up to 15 trips. TripLog Basic lists unlimited automatic GPS tracking, but its reports need Premium or the one 7-day pass per calendar year.\n\nOver 12 months, those caps come to 480 drives on MileIQ Free (40 x 12), 360 automatic trips on Everlance Basic (30 x 12) and 180 reported trips on Driversnote Free (15 x 12). As an illustration, a driver logging 50 drives a month passes all three caps. Everlance Basic still accepts unlimited manual trips after the 30 automatic ones.\n\nNext, compare annual and monthly billing. MileIQ Unlimited costs $139.92 a year on annual billing and $167.88 a year on monthly billing, a $27.96 difference. Everlance Starter costs $89.99 a year on annual billing and $131.88 a year at $10.99 a month, a $41.89 difference.\n\nThen decide whether mileage is the whole job. MileIQ's individual plans list drives, automatic tracking and reports, and Driversnote Pro adds an edit log and a public application programming interface (API). TripLog, Everlance, Expensify, Hurdlr and Stride add expense tracking, while Everlance Professional, Hurdlr and Stride add tax tools.\n\nLast, price out a team if more than one driver needs logs. Driversnote Teams costs 11 USD per license per month for 2 to 10 users, and Expensify Collect costs $5 per member per month. TripLog quotes teams with no public price, and MileIQ for Teams pricing sits on a separate business pricing page."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Everlance vs MileIQ on Free Trips and Annual Price",
      "body": "Everlance is a mileage and expense tracker with one free plan and two paid plans. Basic costs $0 with no payment required and covers 30 automatically detected trips per month, unlimited manual trips, and mileage and expense reports. Starter costs $89.99 per year or $10.99 per month and adds unlimited automatic mileage tracking, expense tracking with receipt uploads and custom IRS-compliant reporting.\n\nProfessional costs $119.99 per year or $19.99 per month. It adds automatic expense tracking, an artificial intelligence (AI) deduction finder, all-inclusive 1099 tax filing and $1 million audit defense. Every Everlance plan has a 7-day free trial, and a paid-plan trial needs a credit card to start.\n\nEverlance fits drivers who want expenses and 1099 tax filing in the same subscription as mileage.\n\nAs an illustration on published prices, Everlance Starter on annual billing costs $89.99, which is $49.93 less than MileIQ Unlimited at $139.92. On monthly billing, Everlance Starter totals $131.88 a year against $167.88 for MileIQ Unlimited, a $36.00 difference. Everlance Professional on annual billing costs $119.99, which is $19.93 under MileIQ Unlimited on annual billing.\n\nEverlance falls short of MileIQ on the free plan. Everlance Basic detects 30 trips automatically each month, while MileIQ Free covers 40 drives, a gap of 10 a month or 120 a year. Everlance Professional on monthly billing reaches $19.99 x 12 = $239.88 a year, the highest single-user annual figure listed in this comparison."
    },
    {
      "heading": "TripLog Free Tracking and Premium Reporting",
      "body": "TripLog offers a free Basic plan and one paid Premium plan for single users. Basic is free forever, with unlimited automatic GPS mileage tracking and basic expense management. Premium costs $59.99 paid annually, shown on the TripLog pricing page as $4.99 per month.\n\nPremium adds unlimited reporting, tax-compliant reports, income and expense tracking, and optical character recognition (OCR) receipt capture. It also adds web app access, auto-classification rules, bank and credit card integration, and connections to SAP Concur and QuickBooks Online. Custom business activities and custom mileage rates come with Premium too, and the [QuickBooks alternatives guide](/alternatives/quickbooks/) covers the accounting side.\n\nTripLog sells optional hardware. The TripLog Drive dedicated GPS device costs $79.99 each, and the TripLog Beacon, a Bluetooth beacon that triggers tracking, costs $19.99 each. The Time add-on costs $4 per month on Premium only, and multi-user pricing is quote-based with no public price.\n\nTripLog fits drivers who want unlimited automatic GPS tracking on a free plan, or tax-compliant reports for $59.99 a year.\n\nAs an illustration, TripLog Premium at $59.99 costs $79.93 less per year than MileIQ Unlimited on annual billing. Adding one TripLog Beacon brings the first year to $79.98. Adding the Time add-on at $4 x 12 = $48 brings a Premium year to $107.99.\n\nTripLog Basic falls short of MileIQ Free on reports. MileIQ Free includes auto-generated reports, while TripLog Basic gets reporting only through a 7-day pass, one per calendar year, available January 1. That pass includes the annual mileage report download, and Premium removes the limit."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Driversnote Trip Caps and Team Licenses",
      "body": "Driversnote lists a Free plan, a Pro plan and two team plans, with prices in USD excluding taxes. The Free plan costs 0 USD per month and includes automatic tracking on iOS, Android and the web, with easy reporting of up to 15 trips per month. Pro costs 11 USD per month and adds unlimited trips and reporting, an edit log and a public API.\n\nA yearly Pro subscription includes a free iBeacon worth 40 USD. Teams costs 11 USD per license per month for small teams of 2 to 10 users, with web tools that let admins collect and validate reports. Teams+ uses custom pricing for 11 or more users and adds single sign-on (SSO) and a dedicated Customer Success Manager.\n\nDriversnote lists support as 24/7. Driversnote fits small teams that want a published per-license price and admin report validation.\n\nAs an illustration, Pro at 11 USD a month totals 11 x 12 = 132 USD over a year. That is $7.92 under MileIQ Unlimited on annual billing and $35.88 under MileIQ Unlimited on monthly billing, before Driversnote's taxes. A 5-person Teams account costs 5 x 11 x 12 = 660 USD per year, and a 10-person account costs 1,320 USD per year.\n\nDriversnote falls short of MileIQ on the free plan. Driversnote Free reports up to 15 trips a month, 25 fewer than the 40 drives on MileIQ Free. The listed 11 USD Pro and Teams prices also exclude taxes, so the yearly figures above are pre-tax."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Expensify Free Mileage and Per-Member Plans",
      "body": "Expensify is an expense app with a free plan for all members and two paid business plans. The free plan includes receipt scanning, mileage tracking, expense reports, categories and tags, custom distance and tax rates, and personal card import. Individuals can use New Expensify free with unlimited SmartScans, though extra functionality such as expense categorization requires a paid plan.\n\nCollect costs $5 per member per month for small businesses. It covers reimbursements, corporate card management, approvals, travel booking, and QuickBooks and Xero integrations. Control uses custom pricing, as low as $9 per active member per month.\n\nExpensify fits small businesses that want mileage tracking inside an expense reporting and reimbursement system.\n\nAs an illustration, Collect costs $5 x 12 = $60 per member per year. A 5-member team pays 5 x $60 = $300 a year on Collect. Control starts at $9 x 5 x 12 = $540 a year for 5 active members.\n\nExpensify falls short of MileIQ on drive detection details. Expensify's pricing page lists mileage tracking without describing automatic drive detection, while MileIQ lists automatic mileage tracking on both its Free and Unlimited plans. Expense categorization for individuals needs a paid Expensify plan, and MileIQ classifies each drive with a left or right swipe."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Hurdlr Premium and Pro Features",
      "body": "Hurdlr lists two named plans, Premium and Pro, with no plan price shown on its pricing page. Hurdlr Premium covers expense and receipt capture, automatic mileage tracking, live tax estimates and reminders. Hurdlr Pro adds invoicing, accounting and core financial reports: income statement, balance sheet and trial balance.\n\nEnterprise requests go through a form on the Hurdlr site. Hurdlr fits people who want automatic mileage tracking in the same app as live tax estimates, invoicing and financial reports.\n\nNo annual cost example is possible for Hurdlr, because Hurdlr does not publish a plan price. Hurdlr falls short of MileIQ on price visibility. MileIQ publishes $0, $11.66 and $13.99 monthly figures, so its yearly cost of $0, $139.92 or $167.88 is known before signup."
    },
    {
      "heading": "MileageWise Past Mileage Recovery Packages",
      "body": "MileageWise sells packages for past mileage recovery, built from a Google Maps Timeline export. Bronze costs $229 and covers any 1 year. Silver costs $349 for any 2 years, Gold costs $449 for any 3 years, and Gold Lifetime costs $559 for unlimited years, with sales tax included.\n\nEach package includes car trip identification and filtering, plus location matching with addresses and points of interest. It also includes flexible trip editing, an automatic audit pre-check, a web dashboard and a mobile app. Reports come instantly in Portable Document Format (PDF), comma-separated values (CSV) and Excel workbook (XLSX) files.\n\nThe MileageWise pricing page says the package is tax deductible as a business expense. MileageWise fits drivers who need a mileage log rebuilt for past years from a Google Maps Timeline export.\n\nAs an illustration, Silver works out to $349 / 2 = $174.50 per covered year. Gold works out to $449 / 3, about $149.67 per covered year. MileIQ Unlimited on annual billing costs $139.92 a year.\n\nMileageWise falls short of MileIQ on entry price. The cheapest MileageWise package costs $229, while MileIQ Free costs $0 for 40 drives a month. The MileageWise pricing facts list a mobile app but do not describe automatic tracking of new drives, and a manual record can start from the [mileage log template](/templates/mileage-log-template/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Stride Free Mileage and Expense Tracking",
      "body": "Stride offers a free app for 1099 workers to track mileage and expenses and prepare for taxes. The app produces IRS-ready expense reports that can be handed to an accountant. Stride also sells health insurance marketplace and benefits products, which sit outside this comparison.\n\nStride fits 1099 workers who want mileage and expense tracking at $0 a year. The [DoorDash mileage deduction guide](/mileage-deduction/doordash-mileage-deduction/) covers the deduction for DoorDash driving.\n\nAs an illustration, Stride costs $0 per year. That is $139.92 less than MileIQ Unlimited on annual billing and the same $0 as MileIQ Free.\n\nStride falls short of MileIQ on published tracking details. Stride's listed features do not state automatic drive detection or a monthly trip cap, while MileIQ Free lists automatic tracking with a 40-drive monthly limit. No team mileage plan appears in Stride's listed features, and MileIQ for Teams tracks employees' mileage automatically."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "MileIQ and each of these alternatives leave some drivers out.\n\n- MileIQ Free fits poorly for drivers who log more than 40 drives a month. TripLog Basic lists unlimited automatic GPS tracking at $0, and MileIQ Unlimited removes the cap for $139.92 a year.\n- Everlance Basic fits poorly for drivers with more than 30 automatic trips a month. Everlance Starter at $89.99 a year adds unlimited automatic tracking.\n- Driversnote Free fits poorly for drivers reporting more than 15 trips a month. Driversnote Pro at 11 USD a month covers unlimited trips.\n- TripLog Basic fits poorly for drivers who need reports more than one week a year. TripLog Premium at $59.99 a year adds unlimited reporting.\n- Hurdlr fits poorly for buyers who want a published price before signup. MileIQ, Everlance, TripLog and Driversnote all publish plan prices.\n- MileageWise fits poorly for drivers with no past years to recover, since its smallest package costs $229. MileIQ Free covers new drives at $0.\n- Expensify fits poorly for individuals who want expense categorization at $0, because that needs a paid plan. Collect costs $5 per member per month."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on MileIQ Alternatives",
      "body": "These pricing or plan changes would shift the comparison:\n\n- MileIQ lowering or raising the 40-drive monthly limit on its Free plan.\n- MileIQ moving Unlimited off $11.66 a month billed annually or $13.99 billed monthly.\n- MileIQ publishing team prices on its individual pricing page.\n- Everlance changing the 30-trip automatic cap on Basic or the $89.99 annual Starter price.\n- TripLog adding full reporting to Basic or publishing a multi-user price.\n- Driversnote raising the 15-trip Free reporting limit or changing the 11 USD Pro and Teams prices.\n- Hurdlr publishing plan prices on its pricing page."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When MileIQ Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "MileIQ fits drivers who want automatic tracking and auto-generated reports on a free plan with 40 drives a month. That free allowance is larger than Everlance Basic at 30 automatic trips and Driversnote Free at 15 reported trips. MileIQ Free also includes auto-generated reports, while TripLog Basic limits reporting to one 7-day pass per year.\n\nMileIQ Unlimited at $11.66 a month billed annually ($139.92 a year) adds unlimited drives. MileIQ describes Unlimited as the best option for reimbursements and tax-compliant reporting. Drives are classified with a left or right swipe, and the result is a tax-compliant mileage report.\n\nMileIQ for Teams tracks employees' mileage automatically, with pricing on a separate business pricing page. MileIQ is published by Bending Spoons US Inc., according to its app store listing."
